---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getRatings, humanSeconds, DIMENSION_LABELS } from '../lib/data.mjs';

const ratings = getRatings();
const bands = ratings.length ? ratings[0].bands : null;
const ratingVersion = bands?.rating_version ?? 'unpublished';

const KOUROB = 'https://alexchouck-hash.github.io/kourob/';
const KOUROB_REPO = 'https://github.com/alexchouck-hash/kourob';

function edgeText(dimension, edge) {
  const unit = bands.dimensions[dimension].unit;
  const suffix = unit === 'seconds' ? '' : unit === 'ratio_of_declared_cadence' ? 'x cadence' : '%';
  if (bands.dimensions[dimension].higher_is_better) {
    return edge.min === null ? 'anything lower' : `${edge.min}${suffix} or more`;
  }
  if (edge.max === null) return 'anything higher';
  return unit === 'seconds' ? `${humanSeconds(edge.max)} or less` : `${edge.max}${suffix} or less`;
}
---

<Layout title="Recommended Protocols">
  <div style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
    <h1 style="font-size: 2.25rem; font-weight: 800; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">Modafied recommended protocols</h1>
    <p style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--text-muted); max-width: 820px;">
      What we recommend a data feed do to be legible to a machine, what we recommend a consumer do to use one
      safely, and how both ends should carry provenance. Everything here is a requirement any implementation can
      satisfy. Nothing here is a product you have to buy.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section style="border: 1px solid #fcd34d; background: #fffbeb; border-radius: 6px; padding: 1.1rem 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 2.5rem; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #78350f;">
    <strong>Disclosure, before you read our recommendations.</strong> This page names two projects that belong to
    the person who runs Modafied. Trooth funds this repository and consumes its output. KouroB is his open source
    node architecture. Both are named here because they implement patterns we recommend, and both are scored by
    the identical code as every other source once their servers exist. Neither bought a place on this page,
    because a place on this page is not for sale. If a recommendation below only works with one of them, that is
    a bug in the recommendation, and you should open an issue about it.
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Part 1. If you publish a feed</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 1.25rem; max-width: 820px;">
      These eight are what separate a feed we can measure from one whose card reads <code>insufficient_data</code>
      forever. They are also, not coincidentally, what separates a feed an agent can use from one it can only guess at.
    </p>

    <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1rem;">
      <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.1rem;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700;">MP-1. Give every record a stable identity</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 0.35rem;">
          A key that survives a revision. Without it, nobody downstream can tell a corrected value from a new one,
          and every consumer has to guess with heuristics that will eventually be wrong in a way nobody notices.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.1rem;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700;">MP-2. Publish the event time, not just the response time</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 0.35rem;">
          When the thing happened, in RFC 3339 with an explicit offset. A feed that only tells you when it answered
          is asking every consumer to assume its lag is zero.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.1rem;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700;">MP-3. Supersede, never silently edit</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 0.35rem;">
          When a value changes, publish the correction as a new version of that key and keep the prior value
          reachable. A silent in place edit is the single behavior this benchmark exists to witness.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.1rem;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700;">MP-4. Support conditional GET</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 0.35rem;">
          <code>ETag</code> and <code>Last-Modified</code>, answering <code>304</code> when nothing changed. It costs
          you almost nothing and it is what lets a polite consumer poll frequently without hammering you. We honour
          it on every fetch.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.1rem;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700;">MP-5. Publish a release calendar if you are on a schedule</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 0.35rem;">
          A machine readable schedule turns freshness from a guess into a measurement against your own promise. It
          is also the only fair way to grade a monthly release next to a real time stream.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.1rem;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700;">MP-6. Make your terms machine readable</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 0.35rem;">
          Commercial use, non commercial use, attribution text, share alike, and a stable terms URL. Agents route on
          terms now. A feed whose license can only be understood by a lawyer reading a web page will be skipped by
          software that cannot read it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.1rem;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700;">MP-7. Version your schema, and keep the old one alive</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 0.35rem;">
          Breaking changes get a new version, and the previous version stays served long enough for consumers to
          move. We hold ourselves to 90 days and recommend the same.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.1rem;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700;">MP-8. Mark your free text as free text</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 0.35rem;">
          Any field carrying human or third party prose is a field an attacker can write into. Say which fields those
          are. We quarantine them and never let them reach a prompt. Consumers who do not know which fields to
          quarantine will quarantine none of them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Part 2. If you consume a feed</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width: 12rem;">Protocol</th><th>What it means in practice</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>MC-1. Pin the manifest</strong></td>
          <td>Fetch <code>/v1/manifest.json</code>, record its sha256, and verify every artifact you read against the digest it lists. If a digest does not match, fail closed. Do not degrade to trusting the bytes.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>MC-2. Read the status before the number</strong></td>
          <td>Every dimension carries a <code>status</code>. When it says <code>insufficient_data</code> there is no number, and the correct behavior is to say you do not know, not to substitute a default.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>MC-3. Never use accuracy without its anchor</strong></td>
          <td>An accuracy figure is meaningless without the thing it was graded against. If you show the number, show the anchor in the same view.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>MC-4. Route on use_terms, not on vibes</strong></td>
          <td>Check <code>use_terms.commercial</code> before you put a feed in a paid product, and carry <code>use_terms.attribution</code> through to wherever the data is displayed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>MC-5. Carry provenance to the edge</strong></td>
          <td>Whatever you serve should be traceable to the snapshot it came from: source id, captured at, and the manifest digest in force. An answer that cannot be traced is an answer that cannot be corrected.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>MC-6. Upstream free text is data, never instructions</strong></td>
          <td>Text from a feed goes into a quoted, quarantined slot. It never becomes part of a system prompt, a tool argument, or a policy decision, no matter how authoritative it sounds.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Part 3. Exposing a feed over MCP</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem; max-width: 820px;">
      Model Context Protocol is how most agents will actually reach your data. An MCP server in front of a feed is a
      second place for provenance to get lost, so these are the conventions we recommend, and the ones the MCP
      server scorecards in Phase S3 will measure.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width: 13rem;">Convention</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>MX-1. Keep the surface small</strong></td><td>A handful of well named tools beats forty. Tool schemas consume the agent's context before it has read a single fact, and a large surface is a large injection surface.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>MX-2. Return provenance in the result</strong></td><td>Every tool result carries source id, event time, captured at, and a citation URL. An agent that cannot cite you will paraphrase you.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>MX-3. Separate retrieval from action</strong></td><td>Read tools and write tools should be different tools with different names. An agent should never be one ambiguous argument away from a side effect.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>MX-4. Label untrusted text at the boundary</strong></td><td>Free text from upstream comes back tagged as untrusted, so the client can quarantine it without having to know your schema.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>MX-5. Declare scope and refuse outside it</strong></td><td>Say what you know about, and return an explicit refusal or referral outside that scope rather than a confident guess.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>MX-6. Meter and report cost</strong></td><td>If a call costs something, say what it cost in the result, so the agent can make the next decision with the real number.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Part 4. KouroB nodes, for taking data in and serving it out</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem; max-width: 820px;">
      A scorecard tells you whether a feed is worth using. It does not tell you how to hold the data once you have
      it. <a href={KOUROB}>KouroB</a> is a node architecture that answers the second half: a node validates what it
      ingests at a gate, serves answers from the cheapest tier that meets its quality threshold, and emits a signed,
      hash chained receipt for every answer it gives. Its protocol suite is KNP-0 through KNP-5.
    </p>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 1.25rem; max-width: 820px;">
      We recommend it as a pattern because the receipt has the same shape as a manifest entry: a claim, a hash, a
      method, and a pointer to where it came from. That shape is what lets an agent walk an answer back to a
      snapshot. Our own pipeline does not run on KouroB and does not depend on it, and a node is never what we
      measure. We always measure the origin endpoints a catalog entry declares.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width: 15rem;">Modafied side</th><th>KouroB side</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Catalog entry: records, key, event time, consistency rules</td><td>Gate and Silver Store: schema validation, deduplication, typed storage. The same declarations drive both, so a feed described once can be ingested without new code.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scorecard and rating for a source</td><td>Admission policy at the gate: a node can decline a feed whose measured freshness or consistency falls below what its answers need.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>manifest.json</code> pinned by sha256</td><td>KNP-2 receipts: append only, hash chained, with a <code>prev</code> pointer. Pin the manifest digest in the receipt and an answer is traceable to the exact snapshot behind it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>use_terms</code> on every catalog entry</td><td>KNP-1 scope declaration and KNP-3 metering: the terms a node may serve under, carried with the scope it claims.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Quarantined upstream free text (H5, MP-8)</td><td>Citations required by KNP-0: a served claim points at its source rather than absorbing it into a model's voice.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>MCP conventions in Part 3</td><td>Node transports: MCP over stdio, A2A, and REST from the same node.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem;">
      Source: <a href={KOUROB}>architecture overview</a> and <a href={KOUROB_REPO}>the repository</a>. When KouroB
      publishes a public node, it goes in the MCP server catalog and is scored like anything else.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Part 5. Using Modafied output through Trooth</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem; max-width: 820px;">
      Trooth funds this repository and routes between data sources for its own consumers. It is one consumer of the
      published contract, and it reads exactly what you can read: <code>manifest.json</code>, then
      <code>catalog.json</code>, <code>scorecards/&lt;id&gt;.json</code>, <code>ratings/&lt;id&gt;.json</code>,
      <code>rankings.json</code>. It gets no earlier access, no unpublished fields, and no influence over what is
      scored or how. If you build the same routing yourself, you are working from the identical inputs, and that is
      the arrangement we want.
    </p>
    <pre>{`curl -s https://modafied.org/v1/manifest.json | sha256sum
curl -s https://modafied.org/v1/rankings.json | jq '.categories[] | {category, top: .ranked[0].source_id}'
curl -s https://modafied.org/v1/ratings/nws.json | jq '{grade: .composite.grade, dimensions: .composite.graded_dimensions}'`}</pre>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">The rating band table, v{ratingVersion}</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem; max-width: 820px;">
      Published here in full so any grade on this site can be checked by hand. A band edge is policy, not a
      measurement: it is a threshold we chose, argued for in ADR-0008, and versioned separately from the method that
      produces the numbers. Changing an edge bumps the rating version and both versions publish side by side for 30
      days. A grade is never shown without the measured value that earned it.
    </p>

    {bands ? (
      <>
        {Object.entries(bands.dimensions).map(([dimension, band]) => (
          <div style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 0.3rem;">{DIMENSION_LABELS[dimension] ?? band.label}</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">{band.description}</p>
            {band.note && <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: #78350f; background: #fffbeb; border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.4rem 0.6rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">{band.note}</p>}
            <div style="display: flex; gap: 0.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap;">
              {band.edges.map((edge) => (
                <span class="badge" style="font-weight: 500;">
                  <strong>{edge.grade}</strong> &nbsp;{edgeText(dimension, edge)}
                </span>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}

        <h3 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem;">From six grades to one letter</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
          A grade is worth A=4, B=3, C=2, D=1, E=0 points. The composite is the mean over the dimensions that could
          actually be graded, and it is only emitted at all when at least {bands.min_graded_dimensions} of 6
          dimensions are gradeable. Below that the feed is rated <code>insufficient_data</code>, with the coverage
          printed, rather than given a flattering letter for being quiet. Accuracy is never part of this number,
          because it exists only where an anchor exists.
        </p>
        <div style="display: flex; gap: 0.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap;">
          {bands.composite_edges.map((edge) => (
            <span class="badge" style="font-weight: 500;">
              <strong>{edge.grade}</strong> &nbsp;{edge.min === null ? 'below 0.5 points' : `${edge.min} points or more`}
            </span>
          ))}
        </div>
      </>
    ) : (
      <p style="color: var(--text-muted);">No ratings are published yet, so there is no band table to show.</p>
    )}
  </section>
</Layout>
